A struct tag can affect JSON names and omission rules. That makes a generated tag a change to an external representation, even if no function body changes. Imagine an API where a client distinguishes an absent field from a field explicitly set to zero. Adding an omission option without checking the wire format can silently change what the client receives.

Use a tiny contract test around the exact value that matters. Test an empty value, a nonempty value and a value whose zero representation has business meaning. Compare the encoded bytes or decode into a representation that preserves the distinction you need. Do not use a broad snapshot that reviewers habitually approve without reading; make the intended names and presence rules visible in the assertion.

```go
package contract

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "testing"
)

func TestAttemptCountRemainsPresent(t *testing.T) {
    type Reply struct {
        Attempts int `json:"attempts"`
    }
    got, err := json.Marshal(Reply{})
    if err != nil { t.Fatal(err) }
    if string(got) != `{"attempts":0}` {
        t.Fatalf("wire contract changed: %s", got)
    }
}
```

This is an illustrative complete test. Its purpose is to make a zero-value contract explicit, not to prescribe the right omission rule for every API.

A successful HTTP call hands the application a response body with a lifecycle. Decide which function owns it and where that ownership ends. The function that receives the body should either consume and close it or transfer responsibility through an explicit interface. Returning a body without documenting that transfer makes cleanup depend on callers remembering an invisible convention.

A small helper can keep the common status-only case readable. This fragment is intended to live in a package that already imports `context` and `net/http`; its caller supplies a shared client and an approved endpoint.

```go
func fetchStatus(ctx context.Context, client *http.Client, endpoint string) (int, error) {
    req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, endpoint, nil)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    resp, err := client.Do(req)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
```

The helper does not invent a retry policy or convert every non-success status into a transport error. Those are separate product decisions. Keep them visible at the layer that understands the operation. This separation makes the example easier to test and prevents a connection-cleanup improvement from accidentally changing business behavior. The [HTTP package reference](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http) documents the client and response contracts behind the fragment.

Latency alone is an ambiguous signal. A request can become faster because of a warm DNS cache, a nearby backend, a different TLS path or less server work. Add a connection-level observation when investigating reuse. Go’s `net/http/httptrace` package exposes hooks that can help associate a request with connection acquisition and reuse.

For a controlled diagnostic run, attach a trace and record whether the transport reports a reused connection. Keep the output small and avoid including request credentials or sensitive URLs. This fragment illustrates one observation point; it is not a complete production telemetry system.

```go
trace := &httptrace.ClientTrace{
    GotConn: func(info httptrace.GotConnInfo) {
        log.Printf("http connection reused=%t idle=%t", info.Reused, info.WasIdle)
    },
}
ctx = httptrace.WithClientTrace(ctx, trace)
```

Use the same observation in the before-and-after experiment. Record the toolchain and the negotiated protocol with the test description. If a proxy sits between the application and upstream, be precise about which connection the trace describes. The [httptrace documentation](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http/httptrace) is the source for these hooks. A trace gives the team a sharper question than “the new release felt faster,” while leaving room for end-to-end measurements that matter to users.

A small scalar function is valuable even when the optimized path is much faster. It can serve as a readable reference, a fallback and a test oracle for representative inputs. For a byte-counting example, the contract can be deliberately simple:

```go
func countByte(src []byte, want byte) int {
    count := 0
    for _, value := range src {
        if value == want {
            count++
        }
    }
    return count
}
```

This fragment is a baseline, not an SIMD implementation and not a claim that it needs replacing. Compare any experimental version against it across empty slices, short slices, repeated values and inputs with no matches. Include data where matches occur near the beginning and end. When vector processing handles a wide chunk at a time, the leftover tail still has to obey the same contract.

Keep the reference in a form reviewers can understand without hardware expertise. If both the reference and optimized versions share the same complicated helper, they can share the same mistake. Independent clarity is useful here. A performance test tells you how long one implementation took; a reference comparison helps determine whether both implementations did the same work. Neither measurement replaces the other, and a release decision should retain evidence for both.

A microbenchmark can isolate the cost of creating a small object, provided the result remains observable to the compiler. The following example deliberately makes the allocated pointer escape through a package-level sink. It models one operation; it does not model an entire request or a production cache.

```go
var allocationSink *requestMeta

type requestMeta struct {
    tenant   uint64
    sequence uint64
    flags    uint32
}

func BenchmarkRequestMeta(b *testing.B) {
    b.ReportAllocs()
    for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
        allocationSink = &requestMeta{sequence: uint64(i)}
    }
}
```

Place the fragment in a test package that imports `testing`. Run repeated samples with the same benchmark code and representative toolchains, retaining the full command and environment with the results. For example, `go test -run '^$' -bench BenchmarkRequestMeta -benchmem -count=8` can be a starting command in that package.

Check the allocation count instead of assuming that a source-level `&` guarantees a particular runtime event. The [testing reference](https://pkg.go.dev/testing) documents the benchmark facilities. Treat the experiment’s output as an answer about this narrow operation, then return to the full service before making a deployment or performance claim.

Passing a context into a function does nothing unless the function uses it at the points where it may need to stop. A worker can observe cancellation while waiting for input and still become stuck later while sending output. Review the full path, especially the transition between receiving a value, doing work and delivering the result.

This fragment forwards integers while allowing its owner to cancel either wait. It deliberately leaves output-channel closure to the coordinator, which may have more than one producer.

```go
func forward(ctx context.Context, in <-chan int, out chan<- int) {
    for {
        select {
        case <-ctx.Done():
            return
        case value, ok := <-in:
            if !ok {
                return
            }
            select {
            case out <- value:
            case <-ctx.Done():
                return
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The fragment is not a complete worker-pool implementation. Its caller must still define the lifetime of the context, the channel owners and the join operation. If processing a value itself can block, that processing needs an appropriate cancellation or deadline contract as well. The [context documentation](https://pkg.go.dev/context) defines the signaling mechanism; the application supplies the lifecycle that makes the signal meaningful. Keep those responsibilities visible in the interface instead of relying on a convention buried inside a helper.

For a slice of integers, cloning the elements gives independent integer storage. For a slice of slices, pointers or maps, cloning only the outer sequence leaves the nested objects shared. That is often correct, but the API should say what level it owns.

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "slices"
)

func main() {
    original := [][]int{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}
    outer := slices.Clone(original)
    outer[0][0] = 9
    deep := make([][]int, len(original))
    for i := range original {
        deep[i] = slices.Clone(original[i])
    }
    deep[0][1] = 8
    fmt.Println(original[0])
    fmt.Println(deep[0])
}
```

This prints `[9 2]` followed by `[9 8]`. The first copy retained the inner slices; the second copied each inner integer sequence. A general deep-copy operation is a domain decision, not merely a utility loop. If an element includes a mutex, a file handle, a cyclic graph or identity-sensitive object, blindly copying fields can violate invariants. Prefer a constructor or domain-specific snapshot operation that documents which objects remain shared.

For byte payloads, a dedicated copy is usually straightforward. For a request structure containing both metadata and payloads, identify the mutable members individually. Copying everything indiscriminately can add expensive allocations while still failing to copy one referenced object that actually needed isolation.

A pool can reduce repeated allocation of temporary objects, but it does not know whether a returned byte slice still points inside an object. Once an object has been put back, the former borrower must stop touching it. A different goroutine may obtain it, or the runtime may discard it; neither possibility is under the original caller's control.

The following complete program copies the response before returning the buffer. Its size cutoff is an example policy for this program, not a universal recommended number.

```go
package main

import (
    "bytes"
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

var buffers = sync.Pool{New: func() any {
    return new(bytes.Buffer)
}}

func encode(name string) []byte {
    b := buffers.Get().(*bytes.Buffer)
    b.Reset()
    b.WriteString("hello, ")
    b.WriteString(name)
    result := bytes.Clone(b.Bytes())
    if b.Cap() <= 64*1024 {
        b.Reset()
        buffers.Put(b)
    }
    return result
}

func main() {
    first := encode("Ada")
    _ = encode("Grace")
    fmt.Println(string(first))
}
```

It prints `hello, Ada`. The returned result is owned independently of the pool. The cutoff avoids deliberately retaining a very large buffer through this reuse mechanism. It does not place a hard bound on total process memory: multiple callers can hold buffers simultaneously, response copies still allocate, and other application objects remain live. For a strict resource budget, admission control and bounded concurrency are separate requirements.

Streaming parsers often avoid allocation by returning views into an internal buffer. For example, `bufio.Scanner.Bytes` returns a token whose storage may be overwritten by a subsequent scan. Processing it immediately is different from appending that slice to a long-lived collection. Saving a descriptor saves a view, not a durable token.

```go
package main

import (
    "bufio"
    "bytes"
    "fmt"
    "strings"
)

func main() {
    scanner := bufio.NewScanner(strings.NewReader("red\nblue\n"))
    var tokens [][]byte
    for scanner.Scan() {
        tokens = append(tokens, bytes.Clone(scanner.Bytes()))
    }
    if err := scanner.Err(); err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    for _, token := range tokens {
        fmt.Println(string(token))
    }
}
```

The durable copies print `red` and `blue`. The error check matters independently of ownership: a parser can retain tokens correctly while silently ignoring a read or token-size failure. Large token requirements need explicit scanner configuration and an application-level input bound, not an unbounded assumption that every line will fit. These are complementary contracts: one controls lifetime, and the other controls accepted input and resource use.

The following program never calls a method through the nil pointer. It prints only the interface's nil comparison and dynamic type, so the example does not rely on whether an Error method happens to tolerate a nil receiver.

```go
package main

import "fmt"

type Failure struct{ Message string }

func (f *Failure) Error() string { return f.Message }

func misleading() error {
    var failure *Failure
    return failure
}

func main() {
    err := misleading()
    fmt.Println(err == nil)
    fmt.Printf("%T\n", err)
}
```

The output is `false` and `*main.Failure`. The returned interface has a concrete pointer type even though the pointer value is nil. A normal success return should instead return the nil interface explicitly. That is a local, understandable contract: if no failure occurred, return `nil`; otherwise construct a valid error value.

Do not fix this by teaching every caller to reflect over every possible nil-like value. That moves a producer bug into all consumers, obscures the contract, and can accidentally treat an intentionally meaningful typed value as success. Repair the constructor or adapter that introduced the inconsistent return. At a third-party boundary, isolate any compatibility workaround and test it against the specific library behavior you actually observed.

The safest repair is usually small. Keep the concrete pointer local to the failure branch, or return nil before constructing it. This makes the absence of an error visible to both the compiler's reader and the next maintainer.

```go
package main

import "fmt"

type ValidationError struct{ Field string }

func (e *ValidationError) Error() string { return "invalid " + e.Field }

func validate(name string) error {
    if name == "" {
        return &ValidationError{Field: "name"}
    }
    return nil
}

func main() {
    fmt.Println(validate("Ada") == nil)
    fmt.Println(validate("") != nil)
}
```

Both lines print `true`. The function's return type remains `error`, allowing callers to depend on behavior rather than construction details. The concrete error can still carry structured information for callers that need it. This is different from returning a typed nil as a signal: callers can now rely on the ordinary nil check before attempting classification.

A useful unit test includes the success case even when the bug report concerns a failure. Error-focused tests often exercise only invalid inputs and miss the accidental typed nil on the successful path. Add the empty, valid and partially valid cases that the domain actually distinguishes. Test the API contract at its return boundary rather than testing only the helper that constructs the pointer.

Even with correct nil behavior, an error API can become inconsistent if some branches return a value and others return a pointer to that value. A method with a value receiver can make both forms implement `error`, but that does not make their dynamic types identical. Classification through `errors.As` depends on assignability to the requested target type.

```go
package main

import (
    "errors"
    "fmt"
)

type Problem struct{ Code string }

func (p Problem) Error() string { return p.Code }

func main() {
    wrapped := fmt.Errorf("save record: %w", &Problem{Code: "conflict"})
    var pointer *Problem
    var value Problem
    fmt.Println(errors.As(wrapped, &pointer))
    fmt.Println(errors.As(wrapped, &value))
}
```

This prints `true` and `false`. The first target requests a `*Problem`; the second requests a `Problem`. The additional address operator supplies a writable target to `errors.As`, which is easy to confuse with the dynamic pointer type being requested. Read the variable declaration first, then the call. This avoids memorizing a misleading rule about how many ampersands a particular example contains.

Choose one exported construction style where practical, document it, and use tests to keep return branches consistent. A value type can be appropriate for small immutable errors. A pointer type can be appropriate for larger structured errors or identity-sensitive cases. Consistency and a deliberate matching contract matter more than choosing the same style for every package in the ecosystem.

Suppose a package returns a value error from one branch and a pointer to the same named type from another. Both may satisfy the `error` interface, but a caller matching one form through `errors.As` does not automatically match the other. This is the kind of inconsistency worth investigating when an analyzer flags pointer-to-error behavior.

```go
package main

import (
    "errors"
    "fmt"
)

type Conflict struct{ Key string }

func (c Conflict) Error() string { return "conflict: " + c.Key }

func main() {
    err := fmt.Errorf("store: %w", &Conflict{Key: "alpha"})
    var value Conflict
    var pointer *Conflict
    fmt.Println(errors.As(err, &value))
    fmt.Println(errors.As(err, &pointer))
}
```

The output is `false` and `true`. This experiment is useful even before opening an editor because it establishes the observable behavior. The question for a maintainer is then which form the package intends to expose and which callers depend on that choice. Do not change exported error construction solely to silence a diagnostic without reviewing compatibility.

The [typed nil and error contracts guide](/news/go-typed-nil-errors-as-contracts) follows the distinction through successful returns, wrapping and HTTP adapters. It is a companion explanation, not a claim that a language-server analyzer validates every branch of an application's error policy.

The following `limiter.go` is a complete source file. Its buffered channel represents occupied execution slots. The send uses a `default` branch, so a full gate refuses work immediately instead of parking another handler. A deferred receive returns the slot after the protected handler exits.

```go
package backpressure

import (
	"context"
	"net/http"
	"time"
)

// Limit bounds simultaneous calls to next. It creates no application wait queue.
// Construct it once, then reuse the returned handler for all requests.
func Limit(max int, next http.Handler) http.Handler {
	if max < 1 || next == nil {
		panic("Limit needs a positive capacity and a handler")
	}
	slots := make(chan struct{}, max)
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		if err := r.Context().Err(); err != nil {
			if err == context.DeadlineExceeded {
				http.Error(w, "work budget exhausted", http.StatusGatewayTimeout)
			}
			return
		}
		select {
		case slots <- struct{}{}:
			defer func() { <-slots }()
		default:
			w.Header().Set("Retry-After", "1")
			http.Error(w, "service busy", http.StatusServiceUnavailable)
			return
		}
		next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
	})
}

// WithBudget propagates one handler-work budget. It does not interrupt a
// non-cooperating handler or impose a socket write deadline.
func WithBudget(budget time.Duration, next http.Handler) http.Handler {
	if budget <= 0 || next == nil {
		panic("WithBudget needs a positive duration and a handler")
	}
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(r.Context(), budget)
		defer cancel()
		next.ServeHTTP(w, r.WithContext(ctx))
	})
}
```

Construct `Limit` during service setup. The gate stays held through the call to `next.ServeHTTP`, including response encoding. This is deliberately a conservative boundary. If slow response delivery should have a separate budget, introduce that boundary explicitly rather than releasing the dependency allowance while detached work still uses it.

Here, 503 means the service cannot currently admit the operation. A per-customer quota might use 429 instead. The `Retry-After` value is a hint for cooperative clients; it neither schedules a retry nor makes repeated writes safe. Authentication, route selection, cheap validation and edge protection should be arranged for your application so rejected traffic cannot consume an unlimited amount of work outside the gate.

The early context check avoids knowingly starting an already-canceled request. It is not an atomic guarantee against cancellation arriving one instruction later. Protected work must still honor its context. A zero capacity is rejected as configuration failure rather than producing a permanently unusable service.

`WithBudget` derives a context before entering the limiter. A shorter parent deadline remains shorter; each layer must not restart a fresh full allowance. The context is a cancellation signal for cooperating operations, not an instruction that can forcibly stop arbitrary CPU work or an uninterruptible library call. This distinction is part of the [official context contract](https://pkg.go.dev/context).

The next complete file, `quote.go`, accepts an injected lookup function. That keeps the business boundary testable without pretending a sleep is a real database. It validates a small identifier, classifies cancellation separately from ordinary dependency failure, and returns a small JSON result.

```go
package backpressure

import (
	"context"
	"encoding/json"
	"errors"
	"net/http"
)

type Quote struct {
	ID    string `json:"id"`
	Cents int64  `json:"cents"`
}

type Lookup func(context.Context, string) (Quote, error)

// QuoteHandler is deliberately small: a production service should also log
// classified dependency errors and response-write errors without leaking them.
func QuoteHandler(lookup Lookup) http.Handler {
	if lookup == nil {
		panic("QuoteHandler needs a lookup function")
	}
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		if r.Method != http.MethodGet {
			w.Header().Set("Allow", http.MethodGet)
			http.Error(w, "method not allowed", http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)
			return
		}
		id := r.URL.Query().Get("id")
		if len(id) == 0 || len(id) > 64 {
			http.Error(w, "id must contain 1 to 64 bytes", http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
		quote, err := lookup(r.Context(), id)
		ctxErr := r.Context().Err()
		switch {
		case errors.Is(ctxErr, context.Canceled):
			return // The caller went away; do not create replacement work.
		case errors.Is(ctxErr, context.DeadlineExceeded):
			http.Error(w, "work budget exhausted", http.StatusGatewayTimeout)
			return
		case err != nil:
			http.Error(w, "lookup failed", http.StatusBadGateway)
			return
		}
		w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
		_ = json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(quote)
	})
}
```

The handler checks its request context after the lookup returns, including the case where a dependency returns a value after cancellation. It does not disclose an internal error string to the caller. A production adapter should record a useful classified error and response-write failures in its observability path. The deliberately compact example does not implement authentication, business-specific missing-item semantics, or a universal error envelope.

The illustration here depicts a shared budget across stages. A work deadline still does not replace socket timeouts, body-size limits or a protocol for partially completed side effects. Review [the HTTP request lifecycle](/learn/services.http-lifecycle) when connecting this handler to a real client or database driver.

The complete companion lives in [docs/examples/go-jsonv2-migration](https://github.com/sergii-ziborov/GopherForge/tree/main/docs/examples/go-jsonv2-migration). Its comparison program uses the same `Listing` type for both packages and prints the results. It also demonstrates omission and duplicate-name handling. The small `must` helper is confined to this executable; application boundaries should return and classify errors.

```go
package main

import (
	jsonv1 "encoding/json"
	jsonv2 "encoding/json/v2"
	"fmt"

	migration "example.com/jsonv2migration"
)

// must is only for this small comparison program.
func must(b []byte, err error) string {
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return string(b)
}

func main() {
	v := migration.Listing{}
	fmt.Println("v1:", must(jsonv1.Marshal(v)))
	fmt.Println("v2:", must(jsonv2.Marshal(v)))
	fmt.Println("v2 compatible:", must(migration.MarshalCompatible(v)))
	fmt.Println("v2 empty items:", must(migration.MarshalEmptyItems(v)))

	type Flags struct {
		Count   int  `json:"count,omitempty"`
		Enabled bool `json:"enabled,omitempty"`
	}
	fmt.Println("v1 omission:", must(jsonv1.Marshal(Flags{})))
	fmt.Println("v2 omission:", must(jsonv2.Marshal(Flags{})))

	type Role struct {
		Role string `json:"role"`
	}
	var old, next Role
	duplicate := []byte(`{"role":"viewer","role":"admin"}`)
	oldErr := jsonv1.Unmarshal(duplicate, &old)
	nextErr := jsonv2.Unmarshal(duplicate, &next)
	fmt.Printf("v1 duplicate: accepted=%t role=%s\n", oldErr == nil, old.Role)
	fmt.Printf("v2 duplicate: accepted=%t\n", nextErr == nil)
}
```

The expected output is checked against the program run, rather than guessed from the type definitions:

```text
v1: {"items":null,"labels":null}
v2: {"items":[],"labels":{}}
v2 compatible: {"items":null,"labels":null}
v2 empty items: {"items":[],"labels":null}
v1 omission: {}
v2 omission: {"count":0,"enabled":false}
v1 duplicate: accepted=true role=admin
v2 duplicate: accepted=false
```

Use this program as a conversation starter with a consumer owner. Ask which output they require and which inputs they actually send. Then replace the fictional type with a small exported response type from your service. Keep that experiment separate from a repository-wide import rewrite so unexpected differences remain easy to locate.

The compatibility options allow a smaller first step. `jsonv1.DefaultOptionsV1()` selects v1 behavior through the v2 API. Later options override earlier settings, so a second helper can opt into one deliberate difference while retaining the rest of that baseline. Our example changes only nil-slice formatting in `MarshalEmptyItems`.

The following complete `contract.go` also contains the strict full-update decoder used by the tests:

```go
package migration

import (
	jsonv1 "encoding/json"
	jsonv2 "encoding/json/v2"
	"fmt"
	"strings"
)

type Listing struct {
	Items  []string          `json:"items"`
	Labels map[string]string `json:"labels"`
}

// MarshalCompatible moves to the v2 API while retaining v1 behavior.
func MarshalCompatible(v Listing) ([]byte, error) {
	return jsonv2.Marshal(v, jsonv1.DefaultOptionsV1())
}

// MarshalEmptyItems opts into one deliberate contract change.
func MarshalEmptyItems(v Listing) ([]byte, error) {
	return jsonv2.Marshal(v,
		jsonv1.DefaultOptionsV1(),
		jsonv2.FormatNilSliceAsNull(false),
	)
}

type Update struct {
	Name    string `json:"name"`
	Enabled bool   `json:"enabled"`
}

// DecodeUpdate publishes a value only after syntax, shape and domain checks.
// The caller must bound data size before decoding. This is a full update,
// not a PATCH representation that distinguishes absent, null and false.
func DecodeUpdate(data []byte) (Update, error) {
	var next Update
	if err := jsonv2.Unmarshal(data, &next,
		jsonv2.RejectUnknownMembers(true)); err != nil {
		return Update{}, fmt.Errorf("decode update: %w", err)
	}
	if strings.TrimSpace(next.Name) == "" {
		return Update{}, fmt.Errorf("name must not be blank")
	}
	return next, nil
}
```

Keep the compatibility choice near the boundary that owns it. An existing public response, a newly designed internal message and a persisted export may need different policies. A single global serializer helper that silently changes all three makes ownership harder to follow.

The [v1 reference](https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json@go1.27.3) lists the legacy options. Review the ones relevant to your actual types, including custom methods and ordered output, rather than treating compatibility mode as a permanent reason to stop investigating. It provides a controlled baseline from which to make a smaller semantic change.

The following original test runs against a local TLS test server. It checks a trusted connection, a deliberately untrusted root pool and a wrong expected hostname. It does not reproduce the eMudhra compromise or prove that a binary includes the incident patch. Its purpose is narrower: catch a transport configuration that starts accepting everything during troubleshooting.

```go
package trustcheck

import (
	"crypto/x509"
	"errors"
	"io"
	"net/http"
	"net/http/httptest"
	"testing"
	"time"
)

func TestTLSVerificationControls(t *testing.T) {
	server := httptest.NewTLSServer(http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		_, _ = io.WriteString(w, "ready")
	}))
	defer server.Close()

	cases := []struct {
		name      string
		change    func(*http.Transport)
		wantError bool
		errorKind string
	}{
		{name: "trusted test certificate"},
		{
			name: "unknown authority",
			change: func(tr *http.Transport) {
				tr.TLSClientConfig.RootCAs = x509.NewCertPool()
			},
			wantError: true,
			errorKind: "authority",
		},
		{
			name: "wrong hostname",
			change: func(tr *http.Transport) {
				tr.TLSClientConfig.ServerName = "wrong.invalid"
			},
			wantError: true,
			errorKind: "hostname",
		},
	}
	for _, tc := range cases {
		t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			tr := server.Client().Transport.(*http.Transport).Clone()
			tr.TLSClientConfig = tr.TLSClientConfig.Clone()
			if tc.change != nil {
				tc.change(tr)
			}
			defer tr.CloseIdleConnections()
			client := &http.Client{Transport: tr, Timeout: 3 * time.Second}
			response, err := client.Get(server.URL)
			if response != nil {
				defer response.Body.Close()
			}
			if (err != nil) != tc.wantError {
				t.Fatalf("error = %v; wantError = %v", err, tc.wantError)
			}
			if tc.errorKind == "authority" {
				var target x509.UnknownAuthorityError
				if !errors.As(err, &target) {
					t.Fatalf("want unknown authority, got %v", err)
				}
			}
			if tc.errorKind == "hostname" {
				var target x509.HostnameError
				if !errors.As(err, &target) {
					t.Fatalf("want hostname mismatch, got %v", err)
				}
			}
		})
	}
}
```

Run it with `go test ./...` in a small native Go module. For an application, adapt the same assertions to its real client constructor instead of testing only a newly invented transport. Keep the test local and deterministic. Do not turn an external incident test site into a required dependency of every unit-test run. The standard [httptest reference](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http/httptest#NewTLSServer) describes the local server and its trusted client.

A query has not necessarily finished using its connection when QueryContext returns. The application may still be reading result rows. A repository that returns a raw Rows object transfers cleanup responsibility to its caller, whether the caller understands that contract or not. A repository that materializes a bounded result can keep that responsibility local.

```go
func loadIDs(ctx context.Context, db *sql.DB) ([]int64, error) {
    rows, err := db.QueryContext(ctx,
        `SELECT id FROM orders ORDER BY id LIMIT 100`)
    if err != nil { return nil, err }
    defer rows.Close()

    ids := make([]int64, 0, 100)
    for rows.Next() {
        var id int64
        if err := rows.Scan(&id); err != nil { return nil, err }
        ids = append(ids, id)
    }
    if err := rows.Err(); err != nil { return nil, err }
    return ids, nil
}
```

Here the SQL limit bounds the result size, iteration checks conversion errors, and Rows.Err checks failures that arrive during iteration. The deferred Close protects early-return paths. For write statements that return rows, explicitly consider errors from closing as well; cleanup is not a substitute for checking the operation's outcome.

Avoid performing a slow remote call inside the iteration loop while the database connection remains occupied. If streaming is an intentional requirement, give it its own capacity and cancellation policy. Do not eagerly load an unbounded table merely to release the connection sooner: that trades connection pressure for memory pressure. Pagination, bounded batches and a clear result owner are usually a more useful starting point.

A transaction owns a connection until it finishes. Code that begins a transaction and then calls the original DB handle for a supposedly related operation can acquire another connection and execute outside the transaction. With a one-connection pool it may wait for the connection it is itself holding. With a larger pool it may silently lose atomicity instead of visibly hanging.

```go
func reserve(ctx context.Context, db *sql.DB, sku string) error {
    tx, err := db.BeginTx(ctx, nil)
    if err != nil { return err }
    defer tx.Rollback()

    result, err := tx.ExecContext(ctx,
        `UPDATE stock SET available = available - 1
         WHERE sku = $1 AND available > 0`, sku)
    if err != nil { return err }
    n, err := result.RowsAffected()
    if err != nil { return err }
    if n != 1 { return errors.New("item unavailable") }
    return tx.Commit()
}
```

This PostgreSQL example uses a parameter for the SKU and performs the condition and update in the same statement. It deliberately does not send a notification or call a payment provider inside the transaction. The deferred rollback handles early exits; a successful Commit finishes the transaction first. Review any transaction helper carefully so that it does not hide the commit error or report success before commit completes.

For a multi-step operation, pass an explicit transaction-capable interface or repository bound to that transaction. The [transaction-owner lesson](/learn/sql.transaction-owner) explores that boundary. The official [transaction guide](https://go.dev/doc/database/execute-transactions) is the API reference; your business invariant determines which statements must share the boundary.

The following function owns one listener and one HTTP server. The caller owns signal registration and supplies a context cancelled on the first termination signal. A separate readiness handler can read the same atomic flag. This example intentionally excludes queue workers, WebSockets, routing preparation and database cleanup; those require explicit additional owners.

```go
func serve(ctx context.Context, ln net.Listener,
    handler http.Handler, ready *atomic.Bool) error {
    srv := &http.Server{
        Handler: handler,
        ReadHeaderTimeout: 5 * time.Second,
        IdleTimeout: 60 * time.Second,
    }
    stopped := make(chan error, 1)
    ready.Store(true)
    go func() { stopped <- srv.Serve(ln) }()

    select {
    case err := <-stopped:
        ready.Store(false)
        if errors.Is(err, http.ErrServerClosed) { return nil }
        return err
    case <-ctx.Done():
    }

    ready.Store(false)
    drain, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 25*time.Second)
    defer cancel()
    shutdownErr := srv.Shutdown(drain)
    if shutdownErr != nil { _ = srv.Close() }
    serveErr := <-stopped
    if errors.Is(serveErr, http.ErrServerClosed) { serveErr = nil }
    return errors.Join(shutdownErr, serveErr)
}
```

The buffered result channel prevents the serving goroutine from being stranded if the lifecycle owner is temporarily elsewhere. The owner waits for Shutdown itself, not merely for Serve to return. This distinction matters because the serving loop can stop before active handlers have completed. Read the [Server.Shutdown contract](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#Server.Shutdown) together with the code rather than treating the function name as a complete lifecycle specification.

The twenty-five-second timeout is deliberately visible. Production code should make its relationship to the external grace budget configurable and documented. If Shutdown times out, Close does not prove that arbitrary handler goroutines or their side effects have finished; cooperative cancellation and durable outcomes still need separate design.

This helper creates one timer and one goroutine. Its caller owns cancellation. The goroutine owns closing the result channel and stops its timer on every return path. A successful notification uses an unbuffered send so that the receiver's acceptance is explicit; the send also observes cancellation if the receiver has left.

```go
func delayed(ctx context.Context, after time.Duration) <-chan string {
    out := make(chan string)
    go func() {
        defer close(out)
        timer := time.NewTimer(after)
        defer timer.Stop()
        select {
        case <-ctx.Done():
            return
        case <-timer.C:
        }
        select {
        case out <- "ready":
        case <-ctx.Done():
        }
    }()
    return out
}
```

The helper does not promise which outcome wins if cancellation and delivery become possible at the same instant. That is a separate API design decision. If a business operation needs stronger ordering, represent the decision with a single owner and an explicit state transition rather than assuming select gives cancellation priority.

A buffered output could also be reasonable, but it would change the completion and delivery contract. Choose it because callers benefit from that behavior, not merely because a test otherwise hangs. Similarly, closing the channel from the caller would violate the producer's ownership and could panic when the worker sends. The small helper is useful because these decisions remain visible.

The first test establishes that the component has settled, checks that it has not produced an early result, advances virtual time to just before the deadline, and checks again. Only after crossing the boundary does it accept the expected value. The nanosecond is a precise boundary in this synthetic example, not a production latency objective.

```go
func TestDelayedBoundary(t *testing.T) {
    synctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {
        ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
        defer cancel()
        out := delayed(ctx, 5*time.Second)
        assertEmpty := func() {
            t.Helper()
            select {
            case v, ok := <-out:
                t.Fatalf("early result: %q open=%v", v, ok)
            default:
            }
        }
        synctest.Wait()
        assertEmpty()
        time.Sleep(5*time.Second - time.Nanosecond)
        synctest.Wait()
        assertEmpty()
        time.Sleep(time.Nanosecond)
        synctest.Wait()
        if v, ok := <-out; !ok || v != "ready" {
            t.Fatalf("result=%q open=%v", v, ok)
        }
        if _, ok := <-out; ok { t.Fatal("extra result") }
    })
}
```

Those sleeps advance virtual time inside the bubble; they are not wall-clock guesses about how long a goroutine might take to start. Wait is used to observe a settled state before making a nonblocking assertion. The final channel receive also makes completion visible. Removing that ownership discipline and merely keeping the same durations would not produce an equivalent test.

If the first assertion fails after changing the implementation to send immediately, the test has detected the intended early-delivery defect. That is much stronger evidence than observing that the original implementation passes repeatedly.

Cancellation is a request. A reliable test should also establish that the worker acted on it. Here the producer's closed result channel provides that evidence. The test cancels before the timer is due and then verifies that the channel is closed without a delivered value.

```go
func TestDelayedCancellation(t *testing.T) {
    synctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {
        ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
        defer cancel()
        out := delayed(ctx, time.Hour)
        synctest.Wait()
        cancel()
        synctest.Wait()
        if v, ok := <-out; ok {
            t.Fatalf("notification after cancellation: %q", v)
        }
    })
}
```

The hour makes the intended ordering obvious: cancellation occurs before the notification deadline in virtual time. The test does not wait an actual hour. It also does not prove a stronger promise about a race between cancellation and an already-ready result. Keep that ambiguity documented or design it away if callers require a strict winner.

Review every blocking operation on the cancellation path. A worker can observe context cancellation and still become stuck reporting a final error to an unobserved channel. A helper can close its output while another child goroutine remains alive. For a more complex component, return a separate completion signal or expose an owned StopAndWait operation, then test that complete lifecycle rather than a convenient intermediate flag.

An independent result can be the correct choice when a small piece of a large input must live much longer than that input's processing window. The [bytes.Clone documentation](https://pkg.go.dev/bytes#Clone) describes a standard way to establish that independence. The lab's helper keeps the ownership decision explicit.

```go
func CopyPrefix(buf []byte, n int) []byte {
    return bytes.Clone(buf[:n])
}
```

Its caller supplies a valid length. A unit test checks both mutation directions: changing the copy must not change the source, and changing the source must not change the copy. Checking only equal contents would miss an aliasing defect. The lab's measurement then answers a different question: whether the large source allocation remains live after the helper returns.

Copying everything is not a universal optimization. If the source already has the correct lifetime, a copy can add allocation traffic and CPU with no useful reduction in retained data. If a result contains nested references, copying only an outer container may leave inner objects shared. Define the intended ownership depth instead of using the word copy as though it always meant recursively independent data.

Our earlier [slice ownership article](/news/go-slices-backing-array-append-clip-clone) covers append and cloning behavior in detail. Here the key is lifetime: a small retained view can be expensive because of the object it keeps reachable, not because the slice descriptor itself is large.

The second experiment asks a narrower question: can a small formatter produce the same string with less execution time? The baseline uses fmt.Sprintf. The alternative uses strconv append operations and converts the completed buffer to a string. Both return a value that callers can retain independently.

```go
func FormatEvent(id uint64, active bool) string {
    return fmt.Sprintf("id=%d active=%t", id, active)
}

func AppendEvent(id uint64, active bool) string {
    buf := make([]byte, 0, 48)
    buf = append(buf, "id="...)
    buf = strconv.AppendUint(buf, id, 10)
    buf = append(buf, " active="...)
    buf = strconv.AppendBool(buf, active)
    return string(buf)
}
```

The [fmt reference](https://pkg.go.dev/fmt) defines the formatting operations used by the baseline. The [strconv reference](https://pkg.go.dev/strconv) defines the typed append operations used by the alternative. This example has a deliberately small output contract. It is not a replacement for general formatting, localization, JSON escaping or arbitrary user-controlled templates.

Before timing either function, the tests compare their results for zero, several ordinary identifiers, the maximum uint64 value, and both boolean values. A faster function that drops a space, changes a sign convention or returns mutable shared storage is not an equivalent optimization. The output contract determines what the benchmark is allowed to change.

The [io.Reader contract](https://pkg.go.dev/io#Reader) permits a call to return useful bytes and an error together, including EOF. A caller must consume the returned bytes before interpreting that error. Our fixture returns the four bytes of tail and io.EOF in the same call. The test also uses smaller destination buffers so the example does not depend on everything fitting in one read.

```go
n, err := r.Read(buf)
if n > 0 {
    consume(buf[:n])
}
if err != nil {
    // Handle EOF or the underlying failure after the bytes.
}
```

A loop that breaks immediately whenever err is non-nil can lose valid final data. Conversely, treating every short read as the end of the message can stop too early. A short read describes the amount delivered by that call, not necessarily the application's document boundary.

Prefer an appropriate helper such as io.ReadAll or io.Copy when its semantics fit. A hand-written loop also needs a policy for a reader that repeatedly makes no progress, destination failures, cancellation and partial side effects. The small snippet above illustrates ordering; it is not a complete production copy implementation. Its purpose is to make a common incorrect assumption visible and testable.

A reader limited to N bytes can present an artificial end at that boundary. That is useful for exposing a section of input, but it does not by itself prove the original input ended there. The [io.LimitReader documentation](https://pkg.go.dev/io#LimitReader) defines the stopping behavior. The application must decide whether stopping means success, an allowed prefix, or an oversized-message error.

For a small document that must fit entirely in a budget, the lab reads at most one extra byte. Exactly N bytes remain acceptable; N plus one establishes overflow. The helper rejects negative limits and the integer-overflow edge before computing max plus one.

```go
b, err := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(r, max+1))
if int64(len(b)) > max {
    return nil, ErrTooLarge
}
if err != nil {
    return nil, err
}
return b, nil
```

This is a deliberately bounded buffering design, not a zero-allocation streaming parser. It uses memory proportional to the configured input limit, plus library overhead. That can be the right tradeoff for small API documents because it establishes the whole envelope before business effects begin. It is inappropriate to give the helper an effectively unlimited budget and describe the result as protected merely because a limit parameter exists.

A JSON decoder reads the next value. A successful first Decode does not by itself establish that the request contains exactly one value. The [encoding/json decoder reference](https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json#Decoder) also explains that decoding may buffer beyond the requested value. Reusing the same decoder is therefore important when checking what follows.

The lab first establishes a finite byte envelope, decodes a typed Event, then asks the same decoder for another value. A second successful value is an error for this single-document API. Clean EOF after optional whitespace is accepted; malformed trailing content is rejected.

```go
var extra json.RawMessage
if err := dec.Decode(&extra); err == nil {
    return Event{}, ErrTrailingValue
} else if err != io.EOF {
    return Event{}, err
}
```

Choose the envelope deliberately. A stream of independent JSON values is a valid format when that is the protocol; an endpoint accepting one object has a different contract. Do not apply a single-document check to an intentionally framed multi-message connection and then conclude that streaming is broken. The application's protocol determines whether another value is useful input or a rejected suffix.

For schema evolution and other parser choices, continue with [JSON compatibility](/news/go-json-v2-migration-api-compatibility) and the [input-policy lesson](/learn/stdlib.json-input-policy).

Scanner is convenient for bounded records, but its default token allowance is finite. The [bufio reference](https://pkg.go.dev/bufio#Scanner) documents the default 64-KiB buffer limit and notes that delimiters can affect the actual maximum token size. The lab's seventy-KiB line fails under the default configuration and succeeds with an explicitly larger token budget.

```go
scan := bufio.NewScanner(r)
scan.Buffer(make([]byte, 0, 1024), 128*1024)
for scan.Scan() {
    process(scan.Text())
}
if err := scan.Err(); err != nil {
    return err
}
```

Always inspect the final scanning error. Otherwise a parser can report a clean partial import after a large token stops progress. Configure the budget before scanning begins, and choose it from the record contract rather than increasing it until one failing sample happens to pass.

A per-token allowance is not a whole-stream allowance. Ten million individually small records can still represent too much work. Likewise, collecting every scanned record into a slice defeats a claim of bounded retained memory. Pair token size with limits on total bytes, record count, processing concurrency and retained results. The resource boundary is the whole pipeline, not just the convenient loop at its entrance.

The correct increment holds one lock across reading and updating the value. The caller asks for an increment rather than assembling that operation from independently synchronized getters and setters. The API now expresses the intended transition.

```go
func (c *Counter) Add(n int) {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    c.value += n
}
```

The distinction becomes more important for a reservation. A check that there is enough capacity and the subtraction of that capacity must share a boundary. If those are separate calls, several callers may all receive a positive availability answer before any of them reserves their units.

```go
func (c *Counter) TryReserve(n int) bool {
    if n <= 0 {
        return false
    }
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    if n > c.value {
        return false
    }
    c.value -= n
    return true
}
```

The tests check zero and negative requests, excessive requests, exactly the remaining capacity and competing reservations. A mutex alone does not establish those boundary rules; the checks and state change inside the operation do. For a real capacity service, also define who releases a reservation and how abandoned or expired work is reconciled. The small lab addresses the local transition, not a distributed inventory system.

Reading the version with one load and the limit with another can observe two different publications. If a reader needs a coherent pair, acquire one snapshot for the operation and read both values from it. The lab's Snapshot method loads one configuration pointer and returns an independent copy of that version's map.

```go
func (s *ConfigStore) Snapshot() map[string]int {
    v := s.active.Load()
    if v == nil {
        return map[string]int{}
    }
    return clone(v.rates)
}
```

The tests update version and limit together while readers verify that limit equals ten times version. Another test mutates the constructor's input and a returned snapshot, then verifies that the store's state is unchanged. The second test catches a different class of defect from a mixed-version read: a caller receiving mutable ownership of internal state.

The [maps.Clone documentation](https://pkg.go.dev/maps#Clone) is a useful reminder that an outer map copy is shallow. Our map values are integers, so copying those values establishes the independence this example needs. If a configuration contains slices, pointers or nested maps, define the ownership of those inner objects separately. Copying one container does not recursively make the entire graph immutable.

For the lifetime consequences of shared references, continue with the [heap-graph guide](/news/go-heap-graphs-reachability-retained-memory) and [atomic snapshot lesson](/learn/concurrency.atomic-snapshots).

The downloadable lab uses only the standard library and synthetic values. It does not add analytics or logging to GopherForge itself. Its logger construction accepts an `io.Writer`, making the output destination replaceable in tests. A deterministic option removes timestamps only for stable lab output; an actual service must deliberately choose its timestamp and retention policies.

```go
func RequestLogger(base *slog.Logger, requestID string) *slog.Logger {
    return base.With(
        slog.String("service", "catalog"),
        slog.String("request_id", requestID),
    ).WithGroup("request")
}
```

The lab then emits typed attributes for route, status, elapsed milliseconds and classified outcome. A successful example produces this JSON object:

```json
{
  "level": "INFO",
  "msg": "request.complete",
  "service": "catalog",
  "request_id": "demo-001",
  "request": {
    "route": "/items/{id}",
    "status": 200,
    "duration_ms": 120,
    "outcome": "ok"
  }
}
```

The duration is a chosen demonstration value, not a benchmark. Tests decode JSON and assert names, grouping, values and absence of unexpected fields. They do not compare whitespace or require a particular object-key order. That makes the test sensitive to a broken event contract while allowing formatting details to change.

The [package reference](https://pkg.go.dev/log/slog) documents JSONHandler, attributes, groups, levels and value resolution. Our tests explore concrete consequences of those APIs rather than requiring a custom handler for a routine event. Start with the built-in handler; introduce another processing layer only when its behavior is necessary and testable.

The strongest protection in our example is its small input surface. It never receives headers, bodies, raw URLs or capability links. Removing a query string is insufficient: a secret can live in the path itself. Removing a URL fragment is also insufficient when the application already copied that fragment into another field. Choose a trusted route template instead of trying to sanitize an arbitrary address after capture.

OWASP's [logging guidance](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html) recommends excluding sensitive material and testing logging failures. For this lab, we apply that principle through synthetic inputs, an approved schema and two deliberately limited redaction mechanisms. Neither is advertised as a universal personal-data detector.

```go
type Secret string

func (Secret) LogValue() slog.Value {
    return slog.StringValue("[REDACTED]")
}
```

A typed `Secret` passed as a value resolves to a redacted representation. The test also logs `string(Secret("synthetic-plaintext"))` under an ordinary field name and verifies that the plaintext remains. That is an intentional counterexample: conversion discarded the protective behavior. It uses fabricated data and demonstrates why a type cannot prevent callers from copying the original text elsewhere.

The second mechanism replaces explicitly named attributes such as `authorization`, `password` and `token`, case-insensitively, including attributes inside groups. This protects those known names in this output handler. It does not inspect every string, recursively understand arbitrary application objects or repair a message that already contains a secret. Treat it as a defense behind the schema, not permission to log whole objects.

Suppose an operation has a chosen 240 ms total budget. In a synthetic serial example, admission consumes 70 ms, an HTTP dependency 80 ms, database work 40 ms and response work 20 ms. That leaves 30 ms of margin. These are teaching values, not a recommended production SLO or benchmark.

Creating a 200 ms child timeout after those first stages does not restore the original 240 ms. The child remains bounded by the parent's earlier deadline. Conversely, a smaller dependency budget can stop that dependency while the parent still has time to classify the failure and produce an appropriate response.

```go
func Dependency(parent context.Context, budget time.Duration) (
    context.Context, context.CancelFunc,
) {
    return context.WithTimeoutCause(
        parent, budget, ErrDependencyBudget,
    )
}
```

The lab checks both cases. A child requesting an hour inherits a parent's ten-millisecond deadline. A three-second local budget can expire while an hour-long parent remains active. The tests use virtual time, so those chosen durations describe semantics without making the test suite wait for real hours.

Avoid treating every timeout value as a reservation that adds neatly across concurrent work. Parallel tasks overlap, queues consume time before service begins and response cleanup may have a separate owner. Write down which interval each limit covers, then verify the actual path. The objective is a coherent end-to-end contract, not a spreadsheet in which every helper appears to have plenty of time.

Go's range loop over a string is useful because it decodes UTF-8 while retaining an index into the original byte sequence. That combination is often misunderstood. The index does not become a rune number just because the value is a rune. The following complete program makes the distinction visible:

```go
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    for offset, value := range "A😀B" {
        fmt.Printf("byte=%d rune=%U\n", offset, value)
    }
}
```

The offsets are `0`, `1` and `5`. They identify byte positions in the original six-byte string. Those offsets are suitable for slicing that original representation at decoded boundaries. They are not interchangeable with a browser selection index, a row number in a rune array or an index into a normalized copy. Once a transformation changes the representation, old positions need a mapping or a new calculation.

Imagine a validator that stores `offset` and then inserts a normalized version into a form. Even if both strings look identical, the saved offset may point inside a different sequence. Keep validation errors attached to the exact representation they describe, or return a named unit with sufficient context to translate it. A field called `characterIndex` hides this decision. Names such as `utf8ByteOffset` and `utf16Start` make incompatible operations harder to accidentally connect.

A byte ceiling is common in protocols, indexes and storage records. Cutting with `s[:n]` enforces that ceiling but can stop inside an encoded code point. Converting the whole string to runes changes the unit and adds work that may not match the requirement. Our lab validates the complete input, walks the proposed end backward to a rune boundary and copies the accepted prefix into independent storage:

```go
func PrefixBytes(s string, n int) (string, error) {
    if n < 0 {
        return "", ErrLimit
    }
    if !utf8.ValidString(s) {
        return "", ErrInvalidUTF8
    }
    if n >= len(s) {
        return strings.Clone(s), nil
    }
    end := n
    for end > 0 && !utf8.RuneStart(s[end]) {
        end--
    }
    return strings.Clone(s[:end]), nil
}
```

This function has a deliberately limited promise: its result is a valid UTF-8 prefix within a nonnegative byte budget, and malformed input is rejected even if the bad suffix would have been removed. It does **not** preserve whole grapheme clusters. For `"e\u0301"` and a budget of one, the result is `"e"`. The encoding remains valid while the accent disappears. That is acceptable only when the caller explicitly wants this byte-oriented policy.

If a UI adds an ellipsis, reserve its encoded length before selecting a prefix. Decide what happens when the entire marker does not fit, and distinguish a storage limit from a display preview. A preview should not silently replace the original value used for identity, signing or editing. The lab also includes `PrefixRunes`; its name is a contract, not a claim that counting code points solves every visual truncation problem.

A reader can split a multibyte encoding across calls. A chunk ending halfway through a character is not, by itself, evidence that the complete stream is malformed. Validating every transport chunk independently can therefore reject valid input. The opposite shortcut, accepting every partial tail indefinitely, can hide malformed input at the actual end. A streaming decoder needs state and an explicit final-input decision.

The lab uses [bufio.Reader.ReadRune](https://pkg.go.dev/bufio#Reader.ReadRune) to handle boundaries rather than implementing an ad hoc chunk decoder. It counts only the valid prefix before an error and preserves non-EOF reader failures. Its critical distinction is the decoded width, not merely the rune value:

```go
value, width, err := b.ReadRune()
if err == io.EOF {
    return counts, nil
}
if err != nil {
    return counts, err
}
if value == utf8.RuneError && width == 1 {
    return counts, fmt.Errorf("byte %d: %w", counts.Bytes, ErrInvalidUTF8)
}
counts.Bytes += int64(width)
counts.Runes++
```

This fragment is the loop body; the download supplies the complete function, imports and tests. The test reader varies chunk sizes and returns final data together with EOF. Another fixture returns valid bytes followed by a synthetic failure. Both behaviors matter: a consumer must account for final bytes and still report a real failure rather than rewriting every end condition as success. The [streaming I/O guide](/news/go-streaming-io-reader-eof-limits-json) develops that broader reader contract.
